# Test: `show bgp rib` walks the adj-rib-in while a real peer is still feeding
#       it, and every walk answers.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-5 of spec-record-answers-3-zero-alloc, through the real daemon.
#            A show walk and the UPDATE path run on two goroutines at once, so
#            the walk holds PeerRIB's read lock while handleReceivedStructured
#            asks for its write side. Every dump answers and the table reaches
#            its full size.
# PREVENTS:  the read lock taken twice on one path
#            (plan/journal/read-lock-taken-twice-on-one-path.md). Each show
#            pipeline used to ask PeerRIB.IsAddPath from inside a
#            PeerRIB.IterateSorted callback. IterateSorted runs the callback
#            under that type's read lock and IsAddPath takes the same lock, so a
#            write arriving between the two wedges the walk, the UPDATE path and
#            every later reader of that peer's RIB, with nothing logged and
#            nothing timing out. The RIB plugin then answers nothing ever again.
#
# WHY THE WRITER IS A REAL PEER AND NOT A SECOND PLUGIN. The engine hands every
# command for one plugin to that plugin's single event loop
# (Plugin.bridgeEventLoop, pkg/plugin/sdk/sdk_dispatch.go, "callbacks are
# processed serially"), so two commands to bgp-rib never overlap and a fixture
# built on `request bgp rib withdraw` beside `show bgp rib` measures nothing.
# The UPDATE path is a different goroutine: handleReceivedStructured takes
# peerMu for its phase 1 and gives it back BEFORE phase 2 inserts or removes
# anything (rib_structured.go), so a walk holding peerMu.RLock does not hold
# that writer off. That is the interleaving this fixture reproduces, and it is
# the one an operator reaches by running a dump on a peer that is still sending.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT MEASURE, deliberately: the engine's answer queue.
# `show bgp rib` answers with ONE document whatever the table size --
# showPipeline returns a json.RawMessage (rib_pipeline.go) -- so its answer is a
# head, one record and a terminator, three lines. The queue holds 256 lines for a
# consumer that has not been scheduled yet (answerQueueDepth,
# pkg/plugin/rpc/mux.go), and a streamed answer is 259 lines at the shortest, so
# any streamed answer can be abandoned under load
# (plan/journal/bound-too-small-for-its-own-burst.md). No table size reaches that
# bound here, which is what lets the table be large enough to be worth racing.
#
# WHY THE LOAD LOOP COUNTS. `count` drains the same iteration the document form
# drains and then answers one number, so nearly all of its time is the RIB
# iteration this test races the UPDATE path against. The document form spends
# most of its time building JSON, outside that lock, and a 200000-route document
# would pass the wire message ceiling. So the loop counts, and one dump reads the
# whole document early, while the table is small and the peer is still sending.
#
# ANTI-VACUITY. The walker asserts that at least one dump saw a route total
# strictly between empty and complete. A walk that only ever ran before the
# UPDATEs arrived, or only after they had all landed, never sees that, so a
# fixture whose two halves failed to overlap fails rather than passing for a
# concurrency that was not there. Measured on 2026-08-22: 203 walks, the first
# at 0 routes and the last at 200000, with 197 of them on a partial table.
#
# DISCRIMINATION. Reverting the deadlock fix in inboundSource -- reading the
# ADD-PATH flag with peerRIB.IsAddPath from inside the IterateSorted callback
# instead of AddPathFamilies before the walk -- reddens this test on every run,
# with `show bgp rib count did not answer within 20s ... the walk is wedged
# against the UPDATE path`.

# The peer feeds the whole table as one burst of UPDATEs and then holds the
# session open, so the walks below run against a session that stays up.
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=50s
option=linger:value=true
option=update:value=send-bulk:prefix=10.0.0.0/24:count=200000:next-hop=10.0.0.1:origin-as=65001:max-msg=512:eor=true
# ze has nothing of its own to advertise, so its End-of-RIB is the only frame it
# sends. Expecting it is what makes ze-peer bind a listening socket at all: a
# check-mode peer with no ze-peer-consumed expectation exits before it listens.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# The walker: dumps the RIB while the peer's UPDATEs are still being processed.
# Native observer fixture: plugin/show-rib-under-load.

# ze bgp configuration
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external rib-under-load-walk {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/show-rib-under-load"
		encoder json
	}
	internal bgp-rib {
		use bgp-rib
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 500000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		attach process bgp-rib {
			receive [ update state refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s
expect=exit:code=0
